  "generated_text": "To determine the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity, we start by analyzing the given polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the polynomial:\n\\[\nz^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe recognize that this polynomial can be related to the roots of unity. To do this, we make a substitution. Let \\( w = z^2 \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\nw^2 + w + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\( w \\). We solve it using the quadratic formula:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\nwhere \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\( w \\) are:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the roots of the original polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\). Since \\( w = z^2 \\), we have:\n\\[\nz^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe recognize that \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) are complex numbers on the unit circle in the complex plane. Specifically, they are the non-real cube roots of unity, \\( \\omega \\) and \\( \\omega^2 \\), where:\n\\[\n\\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 3} = -\\frac{1}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\omega^2 = e^{-2\\pi i / 3} = -\\frac{1}{2} - i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nz^2 = \\omega \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z^2 = \\omega^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the square roots of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{\\omega} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z = \\pm \\sqrt{\\omega^2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\omega \\) and \\( \\omega^2 \\) are cube roots of unity, their square roots are also cube roots of unity. Specifically:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\omega} = e^{\\pi i / 3} = \\frac{1}{2} + i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sqrt{\\omega^2} = e^{-\\pi i / 3} = \\frac{1}{2} - i\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe cube roots of unity are \\( 1, \\omega, \\omega^2 \\). Therefore, the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\omega}, -\\sqrt{\\omega}, \\sqrt{\\omega^2}, -\\sqrt{\\omega^2}\n\\]\n\nThese roots are all \\( 6 \\)-th roots of unity because:\n\\[\n(\\omega^k)^3 = 1 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad k = 0, 1, 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
